compbench (2.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * debian/rules: build through dh_auto_build so debhelper can pass
    the cross toolchain when dpkg-buildpackage is invoked with -a
    (e.g. -ai386). The previous direct $(MAKE) call dropped CC= and
    silently produced a native amd64 binary inside an i386 package.
  * CI: build the i386 package by cross-compilation on the native
    amd64 baseline instead of QEMU; qemu-user's i386 emulation has a
    broken UDP/DNS path on current GitHub runner images (deterministic
    "Temporary failure resolving" for apt inside the emulated
    container, while the ARM legs resolve fine).

 -- Robin DUBREUIL <robin@dubreuil.pro>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:22:00 +0100

compbench (2.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a 32-bit overflow in the total test count: a crafted --repeat
    value could wrap the product past the 4096-test cap and overflow
    the result store on long runs. The count is now computed in 64
    bits (regression-tested in test.sh).
  * Validate that the two --hdd partitions fit on the device before
    anything destructive; previously a non-fitting percentage erased
    the disk and then died when the second partition overlapped the
    first.
  * Warn at startup when negative zstd levels are requested on a
    kernel older than 6.15 (mounts fail with EINVAL there and the
    tests are silently counted as failures).
  * Print "Interrupted." (exit 1) when SIGINT hits a confirmation
    prompt instead of a misleading "Aborted.", and stop printing the
    "copy failed" warning when a test is cut short by SIGINT.
  * read_tree: treat a short or failed read as a failed read-back
    instead of silently counting it as success (it inflated
    RdMiB/s).
  * Verbose mode now notes special files skipped during the dataset
    copy.
  * usage()/README: document the single-level form of --zstd (e.g.
    --zstd 5); the README example now mentions zlib.
  * Manual page: replace the raw UTF-8 multiplication signs with the
    groff \(mu escape so LC_ALL=C rendering is correct; document the
    Sec/Red time basis, the WrCPU% sync dilution, the fixed test
    order, and hard-link dataset caveats (README too).
  * Makefile: default CFLAGS now include -fstack-protector-strong so
    standalone builds match the Debian package hardening.
  * CI: add an AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer test job
    and a lintian check of the built package.
  * Bump Standards-Version to 4.7.2 (no changes needed; package is
    lintian-clean at error,warning severity).
  * Add a "make test" target running the loop-device suite, and skip
    dh_auto_test during package builds (the suite needs loop devices
    and root). Previously "make test" hit GNU make implicit rules and
    silently materialized a stray executable copy of test.sh named
    "test" inside the source tree during dpkg-buildpackage.
  * test.sh: add the test-count overflow regression probe, a --hdd
    fit-rejection case, and a SIGINT-at-prompt case; renumber the
    tests (16 was skipped).

 -- Robin DUBREUIL <robin@dubreuil.pro>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0100

compbench (2.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix read benchmark measuring cached data instead of disk: drop
    page cache before the cold read test.
  * Add zlib compression algorithm alongside zstd and lzo.
  * Report write/read throughput standard deviation in CSV (WrStddev,
    RdStddev) for repeated runs.
  * Harden integer parsing (--repeat, --hdd, --zstd) with strtol
    validation instead of atoi.
  * Fix ETA accuracy by counting every attempted test as progress.
  * Add manual page (compbench.1).
  * Add "make uninstall" target.
  * CSV metadata: human-readable force mode, renamed duplicate
    "Archive" label, add Host/Kernel/Date for reproducibility.
  * Refactor test loop to eliminate duplication; unify disk-stat
    readers; poll for partition nodes instead of a fixed sleep.
  * Move large result arrays off the stack.
  * Fix exit status on user abort: declining any confirmation prompt
    now always exits 1 (was 0 at the first prompt), matching the
    manual page.
  * curl: pass --fail so HTTP error pages are not saved as archives.
  * Preserve exact source permission bits when copying (umask no
    longer clears group/other bits).
  * Compute throughput standard deviation with Welford's method
    (numerically stable).
  * Switch to native Debian packaging (3.0 (native), version 2.3):
    add debian/source/format, debian/copyright, debian/docs.
  * Document measurement caveats in README and manual page.
  * Exit non-zero when any benchmark test fails (was: exit 0 with
    missing rows); report the failure count, and error out when no
    results were collected at all.
  * Mount-safe cleanup: umount2(MNT_DETACH) fallback, and never
    rm -rf the temp tree while a filesystem is still mounted on it
    (previously a busy mount could be deleted through).
  * Validate DEVICE is a block device before the erasure prompt, and
    show the disk model/size above the prompt so the target can be
    verified before confirming.
  * Accept a bare "yes" (no trailing newline) at the erasure prompt.
  * Reject configurations requesting more than 4096 tests up front
    (lower --repeat or narrow --zstd) instead of stopping mid-run when
    result storage fills; removes the runtime cap warning path.
  * Warn when the cold read-back of a test filesystem fails instead of
    silently reporting zero read metrics for that row.
  * debian/rules: build with dpkg-buildflags (hardening=+all) instead
    of fixed CFLAGS; the package now gets full RELRO/BIND_NOW, PIE,
    stack protector and FORTIFY.
  * Makefile: keep -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 when CFLAGS is overridden on
    the command line; link -lm via LDLIBS.
  * CI: build all .deb architectures on the same debian:bookworm
    baseline (the native amd64 package no longer builds on Ubuntu
    while cross arches build on Debian).
  * test.sh: check xz/tar/sgdisk/mountpoint prerequisites, cover the
    new device and test-count validation, verify a read-only device
    run reports total failure and exits 1, and check for stale mounts
    and leftover temp dirs at the end of the suite.

 -- Robin DUBREUIL <robin@dubreuil.pro>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0100

